{
  "requirement": "Repository validation MUST continue after a failed check and report every failed\ncheck it finds in one run, each as a {term}`requirements.ci.validation_error`.\nThis is a convention for every validation error; the requirement for each check\nstates which conditions that check reports. A file that cannot be parsed, such as\na malformed `habeas.toml`, `book.toml`, or evidence file, is one failed check:\nits error MAY report only the first parse or decoding problem in that file, and\nchecks that do not depend on its contents MUST still run.\n\nThe code that sequences the repository's checks carries out the first paragraph.\nA repository-validation function that collects validation errors MUST hold them\nin a parameter or local variable named `errors`, and a function with such a\nvariable is a site below; authoring code that collects errors only to abort is\nnot one. After one of its checks fails, a site MUST still run every later check\nthat does not depend on the failed check's result, including later checks in the\nsame loop iteration, and MUST NOT report one failure twice.\n\n::::{sites} error_accumulators\n```yaml\nlanguage: rust\npaths: [src, crates/habeas-sdk/src]\nexclude: [src/authoring/inputs.rs, src/authoring/tools.rs]\nrule:\n  kind: function_item\n  has:\n    stopBy: { kind: function_item }\n    any:\n      - kind: parameter\n        has: { field: pattern, regex: \"^errors$\" }\n      - kind: let_declaration\n        has: { field: pattern, regex: \"^errors$\" }\n  not:\n    inside:\n      stopBy: end\n      kind: mod_item\n      has: { field: name, regex: \"^tests$\" }\nmatch:\n  - \"fn check(value: &str, errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\"\n  - |\n    fn run() {\n        let mut errors: BTreeSet<String> = BTreeSet::new();\n        check(\"\", &mut errors);\n    }\nno_match:\n  - \"fn check(value: &str, discovery_errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\"\n  - |\n    fn run() {\n        let mut problems = Vec::new();\n        check(\"\", &mut problems);\n    }\n  - |\n    mod tests {\n        fn check(errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\n    }\n```\n::::\n\n::::{violations} early_exits\n```yaml\nlanguage: rust\npaths: [src, crates/habeas-sdk/src]\nwithin: error_accumulators\nrule:\n  any:\n    - kind: try_expression\n    - kind: return_expression\n    - kind: continue_expression\n    - kind: break_expression\nmatch:\n  - |\n    fn check(v: &Value, errors: &mut Vec<String>) -> Option<u8> {\n        let n = v.as_u64()?;\n        if v.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n        Some(n as u8)\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(items: &[Value], errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        for item in items {\n            if !item.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); continue; }\n            if item.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n        }\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(v: &Value, errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        if !v.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); return; }\n        if v.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(items: &[Value], errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        for item in items {\n            if !item.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); break; }\n        }\n        if items.is_empty() { errors.push(\"empty\".into()); }\n    }\nno_match:\n  - |\n    fn parse(v: &Value) -> Option<u8> {\n        let n = v.as_u64()?;\n        Some(n as u8)\n    }\n```\n::::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.ci.errors` has three groups of obligations.\n- **Run-wide convention, carried out by the sequencing code.** Validation continues after a failed check. Every failed check is reported in one run as a `requirements.ci.validation_error`. No failure is reported twice. An unparseable file (`habeas.toml`, `book.toml`, evidence) counts as one failed check, and checks that do not depend on it still run.\n- **Per-site obligations.** A function holding an `errors` variable or parameter is a site. It must keep running later independent checks, including later ones in the same loop iteration. These are upheld and reviewed per site, so I did not vet the sites.\n- **Site queries.** The site query `error_accumulators` and the violation query `early_exits` select the sites and the likely violations.\n\n**Tests for the run-wide convention.**\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[failed_checks_do_not_hide_later_independent_checks]`\n  - It checks that invalid names, empty text, malformed input fields and misplaced `{fixme}` blocks are all reported in one run, with checks that normally depend on the failed one still running.\n  - It asserts that the error list has no duplicates.\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks]`\n  - A malformed `habeas.toml` and a malformed `book.toml` are each reported exactly once. A malformed evidence file (`evidence/ghost.json`) and an unknown reference in `src/main.rs` are still reported.\n  - Coverage and nextest configuration failures leave the independent checks running and produce no duplicate errors.\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[git_index_failure_is_reported_once_and_later_checks_still_run]` checks that the failure is reported exactly once and that later evidence checks still run.\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors]`, `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[schema_rejects_each_malformed_field_and_input_form]` and `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase]`\n  - They check that many distinct failures are collected together. These include an unparseable evidence file and an unparseable Rust source alongside other findings.\n  - They also check that independent well-formed checks still run, such as the stale-text comparison beside malformed neighbours.\n- `tests/commands.rs#fn[status_loads_nearest_workspace_file_without_validating_authoring]` checks the CLI case. An invalid `habeas.toml` is one finding, and the malformed evidence file is still reported by `status` and `info`.\n- `tests/references.rs#fn[unclosed_fences_still_report_unknown_names]`, `tests/references.rs#fn[malformed_citation_entries_do_not_hide_later_independent_errors]` and `tests/references.rs#fn[citation_reviews_are_required_current_and_not_orphaned]` check later independent errors after unclosed fences, malformed citation entries and stale reviews.\n- Single-report tests:\n  - `src/index.rs#mod[tests]/fn[an_unreadable_record_is_reported_once]`\n  - `tests/evidence_boundaries.rs#fn[an_unlistable_review_directory_is_reported_once]`\n  - `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[every_review_kind_loads_both_formats_and_reports_each_application_failure_once]`\n  - `src/execution_coverage/check.rs#mod[tests]/fn[accepted_exceptions_cannot_waive_invalid_measurement_and_checks_still_collect_errors]`\n- The `src/sites.rs` tests (`a_violation_query_needs_a_site_query`, `within_cannot_name_another_violation_query`, `failed_site_queries_do_not_repeat_errors_through_dependent_violations`) check that site-query failures are reported once and do not stop independent queries.\n\nTogether they cover continuing after a failure, reporting every failure, no duplicates, the parse-failure cases (TOML, JSON, Rust source) and independent checks still running. I found no obligation without a test with a relevant assertion.\n\n**Site query.**\n- I ran `requirements.ci.errors/error_accumulators` and scanned the matches. They are functions with an `errors` parameter or `let [mut] errors`, such as `inspect_repository_with_progress`, `validate_evidence_file`, `validate_input` and `citations.rs#fn[validate]`.\n- The query excludes `mod tests` functions and the authoring files (`src/authoring/inputs.rs`, `src/authoring/tools.rs`) that collect errors only to abort. I found nothing in scope that it wrongly selects.\n- Functions that collect under other names (`failures`, `diagnostics`, `problems`, `findings`) are outside the text's naming rule, so they are not places the query misses.\n- Its snippets cover:\n  - a parameter match and a `BTreeSet` local match;\n  - no match for `discovery_errors`;\n  - no match for `problems`;\n  - no match inside `mod tests`.\n\n**Violation query.** `early_exits` has match snippets for `?`, `continue`, `return` and `break`. Its no_match snippet is a function outside any site that uses `?`. It would miss other ways of skipping later checks, such as a guard that skips them without an early exit, but that is not a gap for a violation query.\n\nNo gaps found, and there are no acknowledgments to account for.",
